Now momentum is transferred in two ways, viz. by the force acting between contiguous portions of a body and by the transfer of moving matter. At <math>\text{B}</math> there is only the latter kind, and since the transfer of matter is <math>\rho_0\hat{\omega}_0\text{U}</math>, where <math>\rho_0</math> is the undisturbed density and <math>\hat{\omega}_0</math> is the undisturbed cross-section, since its velocity is <math>\text{U}</math> the passage of momentum per second is <math>\rho_0\hat{\omega}_0\text{U}_0^2</math>. At <math>\text{A}</math>, if the velocity of the disturbance relative to undisturbed parts of the rod is <math>u</math> from left to right, the velocity relative to <math>\text{A}</math> is <math>\text{U}-u</math>. If <math>\rho</math> is the density at <math>\text{A}</math>, and <math>\hat{\omega}</math> the cross-section, then the momentum carried past <math>\text{A}</math> is <math>\rho\hat{\omega}(\text{U}-u)^2</math>. But if <math>y</math> is the displacement at <math>\text{A}</math>, <math>dy/dx</math> is the extension at <math>\text{A}</math>, and the force acting is a pull across <math>\text{A}</math> equal to <math>\text{Y}\hat{\omega}_0 dy/dx</math>, where <math>\text{Y}</math> is Young's modulus of elasticity. Then we have
{{block center|<math>\text{Y}\hat{\omega}_0 dy/dx+\rho\hat{\omega}(\text{U}-u)^2=\rho_0\hat{\omega}_0\text{U}^2.</math> (27)}}
{{block center|Now <math>u/\text{U}=-dy/dx,</math> (28)}}
